ncerns (areas to monitor)**\n- **Attention / information accuracy:** \u201cCandidate\u202fB has a relatively weak memory for numbers.\u201d This could affect handling of numerical data such as fuel calculations or altitude clearances.\n- **Crew cooperation / professional communication:** \u201cCandidate\u202fB makes nasty remarks about his colleagues.\u201d Negative interpersonal behavior can erode cockpit cohesion and undermine a constructive crew environment.\n**Why the strengths outweigh the concerns**\nThe strengths directly satisfy the highest\u2011priority criteria for a long\u2011distance pilot (reliability, stress resilience, technical competence). The attention issue is specific and can be addressed with procedural checks (e.g., cross\u2011verification, use of electronic aids). The communication problem is more behavioral, but it is isolated to one observable pattern; with coaching and clear expectations it can be corrected before the candidate assumes regular international duties.\nTherefore, I consider Candidate\u202fB\u2019s conscientiousness, stress handling, and technical aptitude the most critical factors supporting his selection, while recognizing that his memory for numbers and interpersonal remarks must be actively managed."}
